'use strict';
const {course,section:S}=require('./ai-helper');
module.exports=course('ai-api-integration',[
{slug:'request-contract',comp:'m1.c1',title:'Build an explicit model-request contract',before:'Review Python dictionaries and the HTTP request/response contract. No provider account or secret is needed.',intro:'A university help desk wants an assistant to explain enrollment policies. Its application sends a bounded request to a model service; the service is not a source of authority over student records. Begin with the payload contract before adding a chat interface.',sections:[S('Separate the actors and instructions','A model API accepts a provider-specific request, not a browser form verbatim. The server selects an allowed model and task instructions, validates the user message, and constructs the representation expected by the chosen API. A message role describes its position in the model interaction; it does not authenticate the human who supplied its text.','Keep application authorization outside the prompt. A student asking to see another student’s fees is still an untrusted request. The server must retrieve only allowed data before building the model context and must validate any action after generation.'),S('Treat parameters as part of the interface','A request may include messages, model selection, output limits and supported sampling controls. Exact field names, message roles and available parameters differ by endpoint and model version. Record that compatibility in an adapter rather than copying an old notebook into production.','Serialization proves JSON syntax, not provider acceptance or semantic safety. Version the task template and adapter, minimize private context, and test omitted/unsupported settings. A fixed synthetic payload is useful for inspecting structure without sending secrets or incurring charges.')],problem:'Serialize a synthetic internal request and inspect its selected fields.',code:'import json\nrequest = {"model": "synthetic-model", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Explain only supplied public policy."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When does enrollment close?"}], "max_output_tokens": 120}\nwire = json.dumps(request)\nparsed = json.loads(wire)\nprint(len(parsed["messages"]))\nprint(parsed["messages"][1]["role"])\nprint(parsed["max_output_tokens"])',expect:'2\nuser\n120',steps:['Construct trusted task settings separately from the user message.','Serialize and parse a synthetic request.','Inspect the role and declared output bound.'],limit:'The output verifies only this internal representation. It is not a live provider compatibility test; max_output_tokens here is an adapter policy field whose translation must match the chosen endpoint.',mistakes:['Copying arbitrary client fields into a provider request.','Treating message roles as application authentication.'],takeaways:['Validate the caller and input before request construction.','Keep endpoint/model compatibility explicit and testable.'],refs:['stream','structure'],questions:[
['What does JSON serialization of a request demonstrate?','Representable JSON syntax','The provider accepted every field','The caller owns referenced records','The model will answer accurately','Serialization constructs a representation; acceptance, permission and answer quality are separate checks.','No network acceptance occurred.','Syntax does not establish ownership.','A valid payload can yield a wrong answer.'],
['Who should select the allowed model for a restricted support feature?','The application policy','Any query-string value','The last generated message','A browser-stored secret','The server applies the feature’s reviewed model policy rather than trusting arbitrary caller settings.','Caller input needs an allowlist.','Generated text is not configuration authority.','Secrets do not belong in browser policy.'],
['What is the role of the user message in a model request?','Untrusted task input','Proof of identity','A database access grant','A provider billing credential','A message carries content into the model interaction without granting application authority.','Identity requires authentication.','Database access is separately authorized.','Credentials have a different purpose.'],
['A student includes another learner’s identifier in a question. What must happen before retrieval?','Authorize the target data','Trust the question’s wording','Send every matching record','Ask the model to grant access','The server must enforce record access before private data enters model context.','A statement is not evidence of permission.','Bulk disclosure is still disclosure.','A model cannot issue application grants.'],
['A new model rejects a previously supported sampling parameter. What should the adapter do?','Validate model capabilities','Retry the same invalid field forever','Hide every provider error','Accept arbitrary replacements','Endpoint/model compatibility should be explicit and tested before sending an unsupported setting.','Repeating an invalid contract will not fix it.','Hiding errors impairs diagnosis.','Unreviewed replacements can change behavior.'],
['A chat client submits system instructions in its JSON body. How should the backend handle trusted instructions?','Use its own task policy','Treat the body as an admin command','Promote every message role','Remove application authorization','The server controls trusted task policy and treats caller-provided messages according to the feature contract.','Body fields do not prove admin status.','Role labels from callers are untrusted.','Prompt handling does not replace authorization.'],
['A developer sees a valid request in a unit fixture. What remains unproved?','Actual endpoint acceptance','The dictionary has keys','The message list has entries','The output limit is numeric','A local representation test does not exercise the remote endpoint’s accepted contract.','The fixture can inspect those keys.','The local list length is directly observable.','The declared integer is locally testable.'],
['A policy-summary request needs no student identity data. Which context is appropriate?','Only relevant public policy','Every student profile','The server environment dump','All previous private chats','Context minimization limits unnecessary disclosure and improves relevance.','Profiles add unnecessary private material.','Environment dumps may contain secrets.','Other conversations cross privacy boundaries.']
]},
{slug:'stream-state',comp:'m1.c1',title:'Render streaming output as a stateful operation',before:'Use the request-contract vocabulary. Distinguish an HTTP response from the text fragments carried inside it.',intro:'A customer-support answer may take several seconds to finish. Streaming lets the interface show partial output sooner, but receiving a first fragment does not mean the answer is complete or safe to act on.',sections:[S('Accumulate events rather than replacing the message','A streaming transport delivers events or chunks whose exact shape is API-specific. Some carry text deltas, some carry metadata and some signal completion, refusal or error. Ignore neither empty deltas nor terminal state merely because a simple demo prints only text.','Keep the visible draft separate from a committed completed message. On cancellation or a broken connection, preserve a clear interrupted state rather than silently labeling a partial sentence as the final response. A reconnect policy must avoid appending a second independent answer to the first.'),S('Design latency, moderation and accessibility together','Measure time to first content separately from end-to-end completion. Streaming can improve perceived responsiveness without reducing total work or token cost. Announcing every fragment to assistive technology can be disruptive; group updates under the interface’s accessibility policy.','Partial output is harder to evaluate than a completed answer. For sensitive workflows, buffer or gate the material that requires validation, show a useful cancel action and never execute operations just because a fragment resembles a command. The fixture models content and terminal events; it does not implement the network protocol.')],problem:'Accumulate synthetic content deltas and distinguish completion from interruption.',code:'events = [{"type": "delta", "text": "Enrollment "}, {"type": "metadata"}, {"type": "delta", "text": "opens Monday."}, {"type": "done"}]\nparts = []\nstate = "streaming"\nfor event in events:\n    if event["type"] == "delta":\n        parts.append(event["text"])\n    elif event["type"] == "done":\n        state = "complete"\nprint("".join(parts))\nprint(state)\nprint("complete" if events[-1]["type"] == "done" else "interrupted")',expect:'Enrollment opens Monday.\ncomplete\ncomplete',steps:['Process content-bearing events in order.','Record completion only from a terminal event.','Inspect the assembled text and state separately.'],limit:'This finite list proves event-reducer behavior, not HTTP/SSE parsing, reconnect correctness, provider latency or moderation of actual generated text.',mistakes:['Treating a network disconnect as a successful completion.','Assuming every stream event contains text.'],takeaways:['Track terminal state explicitly.','Measure responsiveness and completion separately.'],refs:['stream'],questions:[
['What does a first text delta establish?','Partial content arrived','The whole answer passed review','The request finished successfully','Every usage record arrived','A delta is an incremental output event, not evidence of final completion or validation.','Review may require the complete output.','A later error can still occur.','Usage can arrive later or be unavailable.'],
['Why keep a streaming state separate from the displayed text?','Text can be incomplete','Text always authenticates users','State reduces all token charges','State replaces server storage','A partial visible answer must not be mistaken for a committed complete message.','Text is not authentication.','State bookkeeping does not change billing rules.','Persistence is a separate design choice.'],
['How should a metadata-only event affect the content buffer?','Do not append missing text','Append the string null','Erase previous content','Declare the answer complete','The reducer should interpret event kinds and append only actual content deltas.','A missing field is not literal answer text.','Metadata need not invalidate prior content.','Metadata alone is not terminal success.'],
['A support stream disconnects halfway through an answer. What should the UI communicate?','An interrupted response','A verified final answer','An automatic refund completion','A successful hidden retry','The interface should represent the observed incomplete state and any explicit retry policy.','The answer did not complete.','Text transport does not settle financial effects.','A retry cannot be presumed.'],
['A screen reader announces every character delta. What should the team review?','Grouped accessible updates','Disabling all status feedback','Removing the cancel action','Treating latency as correctness','Accessible streaming should provide useful, controlled updates rather than overwhelming announcements.','No feedback also harms usability.','Cancellation remains useful.','Latency does not prove correctness.'],
['Streaming starts faster but finishes at the same time. Which metric improved?','Time to first content','Completed-answer accuracy','Total token usage necessarily','Provider availability necessarily','Earlier partial delivery improves first-content latency without proving changes to other properties.','Accuracy needs a separate evaluation.','The same output can use the same tokens.','One timing result does not prove availability.'],
['A model fragment resembles a database command. What is safe application behavior?','Validate any action separately','Execute the fragment immediately','Trust it after one more token','Authorize it through UI color','Generated partial text must not bypass the application’s action schema and permission checks.','A fragment is incomplete and untrusted.','Another token is not authorization.','Presentation does not grant authority.'],
['A retry starts a new answer after a disconnect. What is the transcript risk?','Mixing independent outputs','Losing the need for limits','Making old tokens free','Proving the first answer correct','A reconnect strategy must distinguish old partial output from a newly generated response.','Resource limits remain relevant.','Billing is provider-specific.','Retry success does not validate prior text.']
]},
{slug:'bounded-retries',comp:'m1.c2',title:'Bound retries, deadlines and rate-limit recovery',before:'Review HTTP status classes, transient failures and the distinction between a client timeout and a remote outcome.',intro:'A logistics assistant shares a provider quota with several background jobs. Retrying every failure immediately can turn a temporary limit into sustained overload and unexpected charges.',sections:[S('Classify before repeating a request','Treat invalid parameters, missing permission and exhausted budget differently from a transient transport failure or a temporary service limit. A status class is a starting point, not the full contract: inspect documented error categories and safe metadata. Do not send secrets or full private prompts into routine error logs.','A timeout means the client did not obtain a timely response. It may not prove the server did no work. Repeated generation can incur duplicate work or charges; requests that trigger tools or side effects need stronger idempotency and reconciliation rules than text-only retries.'),S('Use a single bounded recovery budget','Exponential backoff spaces repeated attempts; jitter avoids synchronized clients, and an attempt cap prevents endless work. Respect applicable retry guidance while keeping the entire operation within a deadline. One layer should own retries so SDK, proxy and worker policies do not multiply attempts.','Rate limits can count requests, tokens or other units and may be shared. A concurrency limiter and queue admission policy complement retries; they do not create more provider capacity. The deterministic schedule below omits real sleeping and randomness so its bounds can be checked offline.')],problem:'Select capped backoff delays that fit a fixed deadline budget.',code:'budget = 10\nspent = 0\ndelays = []\nfor attempt in range(5):\n    delay = min(2 ** attempt, 4)\n    if spent + delay > budget:\n        break\n    delays.append(delay)\n    spent += delay\nprint(delays)\nprint(spent)\nprint(len(delays) <= 5)',expect:'[1, 2, 4]\n7\nTrue',steps:['Compute a capped candidate delay.','Reject a delay that exceeds the remaining budget.','Inspect the finite recovery schedule.'],limit:'This is a deterministic scheduling model, not a real retry client. It excludes request execution time, jitter, Retry-After parsing and remote side-effect reconciliation, which must also fit a production deadline.',mistakes:['Retrying permanent input errors without changing the request.','Allowing independent retry policies to multiply attempts.'],takeaways:['Classify failures before retrying.','Bound attempts, elapsed time and shared concurrency.'],refs:['retry'],questions:[
['What is backoff intended to change?','Spacing between retries','The caller’s permissions','The model’s training data','The response schema','Backoff reduces immediate repeated pressure by spacing attempts under a bounded recovery policy.','Permissions need authorization.','Retries do not retrain a model.','The schema is part of the contract.'],
['Why add jitter to retry delays?','Reduce synchronized attempts','Guarantee no future failure','Remove the need for deadlines','Make every request idempotent','Jitter spreads clients over time; it does not guarantee capacity or safe repetition.','Failures can still occur.','Elapsed-time bounds remain necessary.','Idempotency is a separate contract.'],
['A request has an unsupported parameter. What is the first useful correction?','Repair the request contract','Repeat it with no limit','Add more simultaneous clients','Suppress the error category','A permanent contract error needs correction rather than unchanged automatic retries.','Unlimited repetition repeats the defect.','Concurrency adds pressure.','Diagnosis needs safe error information.'],
['An SDK and worker each allow four attempts. What must be reviewed?','Combined attempt multiplication','Only the browser theme','Only the response wording','Only the model display name','Layered retries can multiply provider calls unless one recovery budget governs them.','Visual design does not bound calls.','Response prose does not coordinate retries.','Display names do not enforce budgets.'],
['A generation request times out. What remains uncertain?','Whether remote work occurred','Whether the client waited','Whether a timer was configured','Whether the user clicked send','A client timeout records missing timely delivery, not a guaranteed absence of server work.','Waiting is locally observable.','The fixture can inspect configuration.','The click can be recorded locally.'],
['A shared quota is exhausted. What should queue admission consider?','Capacity and deadline policy','Only per-request correctness','An infinite backlog','Unlimited parallel retries','Bounded queues and admission protect the system when provider capacity is unavailable.','Correct requests can still exceed capacity.','Infinite backlog harms reliability.','Parallel retries intensify pressure.'],
['A retry delay fits the budget but the next request may run for thirty seconds. What is missing?','Request execution time','A different color palette','An extra prompt example','The user’s display name','The end-to-end deadline must include both waiting and request execution.','Presentation does not bound duration.','Examples do not enforce timeouts.','Names do not control elapsed time.'],
['A tool-calling assistant can create a shipment. Why is retry policy especially important?','Duplicate side effects','Tokens stop being counted','Prompts become authoritative','HTTP stops using statuses','Repeated execution can create real duplicate operations without idempotency and reconciliation.','Token accounting still applies.','Prompts do not grant action authority.','Status semantics remain relevant.']
]},
{slug:'server-boundary',comp:'m2.c1',title:'Protect credentials and validate the feature input',before:'Review authentication, object permissions and input schemas from API Security.',intro:'A retail site wants a product-description assistant in the browser. The browser should call the retailer’s own authorized feature, while the server protects provider credentials and limits the task.',sections:[S('The browser is not a secret store','A shared provider API key must stay on a controlled server boundary or an explicitly designed short-lived credential flow. Bundling a shared secret in JavaScript, an HTML attribute or a mobile binary exposes it to users. A hidden UI field is not a protection mechanism.','The application authenticates users, checks plan/feature permissions, enforces account budgets and constructs a provider request. Provider credentials identify the application to the provider; they do not replace the application’s own authorization model.'),S('Validate the request before spending work','Define accepted fields, types, lengths and task scope. Reject unknown managed settings rather than passing through model identifiers, tool lists or arbitrary system prompts. Check authorization for referenced product or customer data before retrieval.','Use safe diagnostics with correlation ids and coarse categories. Avoid logging raw credentials and private input by default. The fixture accepts a bounded text-only body and explicitly rejects a caller-supplied provider setting; it does not store or inspect a real secret.')],problem:'Validate a narrow synthetic assistant request without exposing credentials.',code:'def valid(body):\n    return isinstance(body, dict) and set(body) == {"text"} and isinstance(body["text"], str) and 1 <= len(body["text"].strip()) <= 200\nprint(valid({"text": "Summarize the public product description"}))\nprint(valid({"text": "hello", "api_key": "SYNTHETIC"}))\nprint(valid({"text": "   "}))',expect:'True\nFalse\nFalse',steps:['Allow only the documented text field.','Validate its type and meaningful length.','Reject managed fields and blank requests.'],limit:'This checks only a local body predicate. Authentication, request-size limits, abuse controls, secret storage, transport protection and actual provider spending require application integration tests.',mistakes:['Shipping a shared provider key to the browser.','Forwarding arbitrary caller-selected tools or model settings.'],takeaways:['Keep credentials at the intended trust boundary.','Validate feature scope before incurring provider work.'],refs:['retry','structure'],questions:[
['Where should a shared provider API key normally live?','Server credential boundary','A public script constant','A hidden input field','An unprotected source map','A shared secret belongs behind the application’s controlled credential boundary.','Public scripts are inspectable.','Hidden fields are still client data.','Source maps can expose embedded values.'],
['What does a provider key identify in a typical server integration?','The provider-side client','The student’s object ownership','The browser’s trustworthiness','The answer’s factual accuracy','Provider authentication and application user authorization solve different problems.','Ownership needs application checks.','A browser remains untrusted input.','A credential does not validate facts.'],
['Why reject unknown managed settings in a feature body?','Preserve the task boundary','Guarantee model truthfulness','Replace all rate limiting','Make JSON parsing unnecessary','A narrow schema prevents callers from controlling settings reserved for the application.','Output quality still needs evaluation.','Valid requests can still be abusive.','Parsing is needed before schema checks.'],
['A customer submits a product id owned by another tenant. What must precede context retrieval?','Tenant-aware authorization','Only a text-length check','Only a valid JSON check','Only a model refusal instruction','The server must authorize referenced data before including it in provider context.','Length does not prove access.','JSON validity does not prove access.','Prompt text does not enforce storage access.'],
['A browser bundle contains a provider key but minifies its variable name. What changed?','Presentation, not secrecy','The credential was revoked','The user became authorized','The provider budget became fixed','Minification does not prevent users from inspecting a value delivered to them.','Revocation is a provider-side action.','Names do not grant permission.','Billing bounds need enforcement.'],
['A text-only feature accepts an extra tools field. What risk should validation prevent?','Caller-expanded capability','Loss of JSON syntax necessarily','Automatic password rotation','More accurate model knowledge','Managed tools must be selected under server policy, not silently expanded by callers.','The body may remain valid JSON.','Tool lists do not rotate passwords.','Capabilities do not guarantee accuracy.'],
['An error logger writes authorization headers for every failed call. What should change?','Minimize sensitive diagnostics','Publish the logs to users','Retry with the header in a prompt','Remove only the log timestamp','Credentials should not be copied into routine diagnostic channels.','Publishing increases exposure.','Prompts are another disclosure path.','Timestamps are not the secret.'],
['A schema check passes. Which control still needs separate enforcement?','Per-account spending limits','Whether text is a string','Whether unknown fields exist','Whether the body is a dictionary','Input validity does not itself enforce who may spend what amount of provider capacity.','The predicate already checks the type.','The allowlist directly checks fields.','The predicate directly checks the container.']
]},
{slug:'conversation-flow',comp:'m2.c2',title:'Keep conversation state owned and ordered',before:'Use the previous server-boundary lesson. Distinguish an application conversation id from a provider request id.',intro:'A customer returns to an earlier support conversation. The app must load the right history, preserve message ordering and prevent another customer from reading or appending to it.',sections:[S('Own the state rather than trusting the client transcript','A conversation record needs an owner or authorized participant scope, a stable application id and an ordered message history. The server must authorize reads and writes to that record; a client-supplied conversation id is a selector rather than permission.','Store task version, relevant model/adapter metadata and completion status when needed for reproducibility. Avoid retaining private content without a purpose and retention policy. Replaying every stored message forever is neither a privacy policy nor a context strategy.'),S('Coordinate concurrent turns','Two simultaneous sends can both read the same history and produce conflicting continuations. Use the application’s concurrency policy, such as a per-conversation in-flight operation or optimistic version check. Do not silently overwrite a newer transcript with an older response.','Represent pending, completed, cancelled and failed operations explicitly. A provider request id helps diagnostics, but the application conversation id and turn version govern ownership and ordering. The fixture demonstrates an expected-version gate only; it is not a database transaction or a distributed lock.')],problem:'Append a synthetic turn only when its conversation owner and version match.',code:'conversation = {"owner": 7, "version": 2, "messages": []}\ndef append(actor, expected, text):\n    if actor != conversation["owner"] or expected != conversation["version"]:\n        return False\n    conversation["messages"].append(text)\n    conversation["version"] += 1\n    return True\nprint(append(8, 2, "private"))\nprint(append(7, 2, "first turn"))\nprint(append(7, 2, "stale turn"))\nprint(conversation["version"])',expect:'False\nTrue\nFalse\n3',steps:['Compare the trusted actor to the owner.','Require the current expected version before mutation.','Reject a stale second append.'],limit:'Sequential in-memory checks demonstrate the policy but not atomic concurrent updates. A real store must enforce the version predicate at the write boundary and handle failures durably.',mistakes:['Using knowledge of a conversation id as access permission.','Allowing stale responses to overwrite later turns.'],takeaways:['Scope every history operation to authorized participants.','Make ordering and in-flight behavior explicit.'],refs:['stream','tokens'],questions:[
['What does a conversation identifier establish by itself?','Which record is requested','Who may read the record','Whether its contents are public','Whether the model finished','An identifier selects a record; authorization and operation state are separate.','Identifiers are not access grants.','Privacy needs a policy.','Completion needs a terminal state.'],
['Why record a turn version?','Detect stale updates','Encrypt every message','Reduce token counts to zero','Replace caller authentication','Version checks help identify conflicting or out-of-order conversation writes.','Version numbers are not encryption.','History still consumes context if sent.','Identity needs authentication.'],
['What is a useful distinction between application and provider request ids?','Different tracking scopes','Both prove user ownership','Neither can aid diagnostics','Both are model instructions','Application records and remote requests are separate entities with different lifecycles.','Tracking ids do not authorize users.','Safe correlation can aid debugging.','Identifiers are not instructions.'],
['Two customer turns begin from the same history version. What needs a policy?','Concurrent turn ordering','Only response font size','Only the welcome message','Only token display rounding','The system must prevent or resolve conflicting continuations under an explicit concurrency rule.','Font size does not coordinate state.','Greeting text does not serialize writes.','Rounding does not control ordering.'],
['A late response arrives after the user cancelled its turn. What should the app check?','Current operation state','Only whether text is nonempty','Only whether it ends with a period','Only the old request timestamp','A late result must not silently commit into a turn whose state no longer permits it.','Nonempty output can still be stale.','Punctuation does not indicate authority.','Old metadata does not prove current validity.'],
['A server loads all conversations before filtering in the browser. What boundary failed?','Server-side data access','Only context compression','Only model sampling','Only the JSON encoding','Private history should be authorized and scoped before it is delivered to the caller.','Compression does not enforce access.','Sampling does not protect stored rows.','Encoding does not prevent disclosure.'],
['An in-memory version test passes sequentially. What remains unproved?','Atomic concurrent writes','The recorded owner value','The local version increment','The number of stored messages','Sequential predicates do not establish transaction behavior under concurrent requests.','The fixture can inspect the owner.','The increment is directly exercised.','The list length is observable.'],
['A long-lived support history contains obsolete private details. What should govern retention?','Purpose-based retention','Unlimited replay by default','The model’s writing style','The UI’s message color','Retention and context selection should reflect privacy and product needs rather than indefinite accumulation.','Unlimited replay creates unnecessary exposure.','Writing style does not define data policy.','Color is not a retention rule.']
]},
{slug:'safe-output',comp:'m2.c2',title:'Separate generated suggestions from application actions',before:'Review structured request validation, object permissions and completed versus partial stream state.',intro:'A service assistant suggests updating a delivery address. Its fluent message is useful to a customer, but it must not become an unrestricted command to the logistics database.',sections:[S('Treat output as untrusted input to the next component','Generated text may contain mistakes, malformed values or instructions copied from untrusted context. Render plain content through safe text mechanisms and validate machine-readable output against the operation’s schema. JSON syntax alone does not establish business validity.','Authorization remains with the application. A suggested order id can refer to someone else’s order; a syntactically valid address change may conflict with a dispatched shipment. Recheck actor, resource, writable fields and current state at execution.'),S('Make human and system decisions visible','For consequential actions, use explicit confirmation or the workflow’s authorized decision gate. Show what will change and keep the operation id/result separate from the assistant’s narrative. Do not label an operation complete merely because the model described success.','Use refusal, invalid output, unavailable provider and incomplete response as distinct states where the contract supports them. Design a fallback that helps users recover without inventing a successful action. The fixture validates a small action proposal; it is not a production schema engine or a provider tool-call implementation.')],problem:'Validate a synthetic proposed address action before execution.',code:'def permitted(actor, order, proposal):\n    return actor == order["owner"] and order["state"] == "draft" and set(proposal) == {"city"} and isinstance(proposal["city"], str) and 1 <= len(proposal["city"]) <= 80\norder = {"owner": 7, "state": "draft"}\nprint(permitted(7, order, {"city": "Lahore"}))\nprint(permitted(8, order, {"city": "Lahore"}))\nprint(permitted(7, order, {"city": "Lahore", "approved": True}))',expect:'True\nFalse\nFalse',steps:['Check the trusted actor and current order state.','Allow only the documented writable field.','Reject cross-owner or managed-field proposals.'],limit:'These are local decision cases, not a live action, atomic write, full address validator or confirmation UI. Real execution must preserve the same checks at its authoritative mutation boundary.',mistakes:['Executing generated claims as authority.','Confusing an assistant’s success sentence with a confirmed system result.'],takeaways:['Validate and authorize downstream operations independently.','Represent actual execution outcomes separately from model prose.'],refs:['structure','stream'],questions:[
['How should generated action arguments be treated?','Untrusted proposed input','An administrator grant','A completed database change','A verified factual source','Generated values need schema, authorization and state checks before they drive operations.','A model cannot grant application roles.','A proposal is not execution.','Generated content can be wrong.'],
['Does valid JSON prove a proposed business action is allowed?','No, policy checks remain','Yes, syntax grants permission','Yes, if the text is fluent','Yes, if streaming was enabled','Syntax validity and business authorization are independent properties.','JSON does not grant access.','Fluency is not evidence of permission.','Transport does not authorize actions.'],
['What should establish that an address update completed?','The authoritative action result','The assistant’s promise','A displayed typing indicator','A nonempty JSON object','Execution status must come from the operation that actually changes state.','Narrative can be inaccurate.','Typing is a UI state.','A representation is not a committed write.'],
['A generated order id belongs to another customer. What should the handler do?','Deny unauthorized access','Trust the generated selector','Ask only for valid JSON','Write first and audit later','Resource authorization must hold regardless of where the selector originated.','Generation does not prove ownership.','JSON cannot establish permission.','Auditing does not undo disclosure or mutation.'],
['An assistant returns HTML-shaped support text for a plain-text UI. Which rendering choice fits?','A safe text-rendering sink','Direct arbitrary HTML insertion','An unrestricted script template','A browser-evaluated command','The output should be rendered under the declared text context rather than interpreted as code.','Arbitrary HTML can change interpretation.','Script contexts need different controls.','Generated text is not executable authority.'],
['A response is incomplete when the provider disconnects. What should action execution require?','A valid complete proposal','Any first text fragment','A plausible action verb','The previous turn’s schema','A partial stream cannot substitute for a fully validated proposal under the current operation contract.','Fragments may omit required fields.','A verb is not a valid operation.','Current data must satisfy current rules.'],
['A shipment becomes dispatched after the model generated an address suggestion. What must be rechecked?','Current operation state','Only the old prompt wording','Only the model identifier','Only the JSON key order','State-dependent rules must hold when the authoritative action executes.','Old instructions do not freeze state.','Model choice does not lock the shipment.','Key order does not govern validity.'],
['A provider outage prevents a requested action proposal. Which fallback is truthful?','Explain unavailability and recovery','Claim the action succeeded','Invent a confirmation identifier','Silently mark the order updated','The UI must distinguish unavailable assistance from completed business operations.','Success would be fabricated.','Invented ids mislead users.','State cannot be changed by a display claim.']
]},
{slug:'token-budget',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Estimate token budgets without confusing units',before:'Use request construction and history-selection concepts. A token is model-encoding-specific, not always a word.',intro:'A subscription support product must reserve room for instructions, retrieved passages, conversation history and the answer. Character counts are useful rough limits but cannot reliably substitute for the chosen model’s tokenizer.',sections:[S('Count the actual representation','Different encodings split text differently, including whitespace, punctuation, non-English scripts and structured message overhead. Count with an appropriate supported tokenizer or provider usage where possible, and state uncertainty when using estimates. A simple words-times-constant heuristic is not an exact meter.','The context window constrains the model’s usable input/output representation. Reserve output capacity and required instructions before choosing optional history. A larger context limit does not guarantee that every included detail will be used correctly.'),S('Create an admission policy','Define input bounds, requested output bounds and a reserve for framing/tool metadata under the chosen API contract. Reject, shorten or summarize under a visible policy before a request exceeds the budget. Do not silently cut away the user’s current task or the trusted policy.','A token budget is different from a currency budget or a rate limit. Track each separately and relate them through explicit assumptions. The fixture starts with synthetic token counts, not a tokenizer, and tests a simple capacity decision.')],problem:'Admit a request only when measured component counts leave its declared output reserve.',code:'limit = 1000\nparts = {"instructions": 120, "history": 430, "retrieval": 180, "framing": 30}\ninput_tokens = sum(parts.values())\noutput_reserve = 200\nprint(input_tokens)\nprint(input_tokens + output_reserve <= limit)\nprint(limit - input_tokens - output_reserve)',expect:'760\nTrue\n40',steps:['Use declared synthetic counts for every input component.','Add the requested output reserve.','Inspect remaining context capacity.'],limit:'The arithmetic is exact for the supplied counts. It does not tokenize text, verify a real model limit or guarantee provider acceptance; actual framing and feature-specific token rules must be measured.',mistakes:['Equating words or characters with exact model tokens.','Using the whole context allowance for history and leaving no output room.'],takeaways:['Keep token, money and rate units distinct.','Reserve required context and output before optional history.'],refs:['tokens'],questions:[
['Is one natural-language word always one model token?','No, encoding determines splits','Yes, in every language','Yes, after JSON serialization','Yes, when output is streamed','Token boundaries depend on the encoding and text, not a universal word rule.','Languages do not share a fixed word/token ratio.','JSON can add framing and punctuation.','Streaming does not redefine tokens.'],
['Why reserve output capacity before selecting history?','Input and output share limits','History is always untrusted code','Output tokens cost nothing','The browser needs no state','Capacity planning must leave room for the requested response under the model’s contract.','History content needs context-specific handling.','Output can have a separate price.','UI state is a different concern.'],
['What does the token-budget fixture verify?','Arithmetic on supplied counts','The model’s real tokenizer','Current provider pricing','Every supported context window','The fixture uses declared synthetic counts to check a capacity decision.','No tokenizer is executed.','No price source is queried.','No live capability check occurs.'],
['A multilingual request exceeds an English-based character heuristic. What should improve estimation?','Use the matching tokenizer','Force one character per token','Ignore non-English text','Remove all output reserves','The model’s actual encoding is a better basis than a universal character ratio.','The ratio is not universal.','Ignoring input miscounts the request.','Output still needs capacity.'],
['A retrieved passage is relevant but exceeds the remaining budget. What should the app do?','Apply its context-selection policy','Append it regardless of limits','Delete trusted instructions first','Pretend the provider saw it','The system should explicitly select, shorten or reject context under its task and capacity rules.','Over-budget input can fail.','Trusted constraints should not be silently discarded.','Unsent material cannot ground an answer.'],
['A larger context window becomes available. What does that alone prove?','A higher declared capacity','Perfect use of every detail','Lower request cost always','No need for retrieval selection','Capacity is not a guarantee of accuracy, relevance or lower unit cost.','Attention and task quality still need evaluation.','Prices may differ.','Selection remains useful for relevance and cost.'],
['The budget omits tool-schema framing. What is the likely issue?','Underestimated input usage','More reliable authorization','Guaranteed cache reuse','Automatic output compression','Omitted representation components can make a nominal estimate smaller than the actual request.','Token accounting does not grant permission.','Caching depends on separate rules.','Output is not automatically compressed.'],
['A request fits the context window but violates the account’s spending cap. What follows?','Reject or adjust under budget policy','Context fit grants spending rights','Ignore currency because tokens fit','Move the secret into the browser','Technical capacity and financial authorization are separate admission conditions.','Capacity does not authorize spending.','Currency limits remain relevant.','Credential exposure does not solve budgeting.']
]},
{slug:'cost-ledger',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Calculate request cost with explicit price assumptions',before:'Review token accounting and exact decimal arithmetic. Prices in this lesson are synthetic, not current provider quotes.',intro:'A SaaS team wants to know whether a support feature fits its subscription margin. A request’s input and output can have different prices, and averages hide unusually expensive interactions.',sections:[S('Make the units visible','Record the price unit, currency, model/version and effective date from the provider’s actual contract. Distinguish input, output, cached input and other billable features where applicable. Multiply each usage category by its own rate; do not assume a single blended rate applies to every call.','Use decimal arithmetic for ledger calculations and round according to an explicit presentation/accounting policy. Do not round each tiny component prematurely and then interpret the result as an exact invoice. Real billing may include features not represented in a small model.'),S('Estimate before the request, reconcile after it','An admission estimate uses expected input and a bounded output reserve. Observed usage can differ, especially on retries, interruptions or tool-enabled flows. Reconcile with safe provider usage metadata and billing records rather than counting visible words.','Track both per-request and aggregate distribution: median, tail, successful versus failed attempts and model/task versions. A low average can conceal one tenant’s runaway loop. The fixture uses invented per-million rates solely to exercise the accounting formula.')],problem:'Compute a synthetic input/output charge in declared currency units.',code:'from decimal import Decimal\nusage = {"input": 1200, "output": 300}\nrates = {"input": Decimal("2.00"), "output": Decimal("8.00")}\ncost = sum(Decimal(usage[k]) * rates[k] / Decimal(1000000) for k in usage)\nprint(f"{cost:.4f}")\nprint(f"{cost * 10000:.2f}")',expect:'0.0048\n48.00',steps:['Apply separate synthetic per-million rates to each category.','Add the unrounded decimal components.','Project the same assumed usage across a declared request count.'],limit:'The output is exact for these invented prices and usage assumptions. It is not a current price quote, invoice reconciliation or guarantee that repeated real requests have identical usage.',mistakes:['Mixing per-thousand and per-million price units.','Using visible text length as authoritative billing usage.'],takeaways:['State rate units and assumptions next to estimates.','Reconcile bounded estimates with actual usage and invoices.'],refs:['tokens','retry'],questions:[
['Why keep input and output price categories separate?','They may have different rates','They use different user identities','Output never consumes capacity','Input is always free','A cost model should apply the contract’s rate to each distinct billable category.','Billing categories do not authenticate callers.','Output can consume limits.','Input pricing depends on the contract.'],
['What is the status of prices used in this lesson’s fixture?','Synthetic accounting assumptions','A current provider price quote','A guaranteed invoice total','An official spending allowance','Invented prices make the formula testable without claiming current commercial terms.','No current pricing endpoint is checked.','Invoices can include other usage.','Budgets come from application policy.'],
['Why use Decimal for the demonstrated ledger?','Explicit decimal arithmetic','It verifies remote billing','It counts model tokens','It authorizes payment','Decimal supports controlled decimal calculations but does not verify external usage or permissions.','No billing service is contacted.','Tokenization is a separate operation.','Arithmetic does not grant authority.'],
['A rate is per million tokens but code divides by one thousand. What is the defect?','A unit conversion error','A harmless JSON difference','An authorization improvement','An output-quality guarantee','The denominator must match the quoted unit or the cost estimate is wrong.','Representation format does not fix units.','Price units do not enforce access.','Cost arithmetic does not validate answers.'],
['A failed request may have consumed provider work. How should cost tracking treat it?','Reconcile applicable actual usage','Assume every failure is free','Charge only visible words','Delete the failed attempt record','Observed usage and provider billing rules determine charges, not client success alone.','Some failures occur after work.','Visible words omit other billed categories.','Deleting evidence harms reconciliation.'],
['An average cost is low but a few requests are extremely expensive. What should monitoring include?','Tail and per-account usage','Only the rounded mean','Only the UI response time','Only successful prompts','Distributions and account-level attribution reveal expensive outliers hidden by averages.','A mean can hide the tail.','Latency and cost differ.','Failed attempts can consume work.'],
['A team rounds each tiny component to cents before summing. What is at risk?','Rounding distortion','Provider authentication','The message role ordering','The model’s context length','Premature rounding can distort aggregate estimates even when each displayed amount looks small.','Rounding does not validate credentials.','Cost display does not order messages.','Context limits are separate.'],
['A provider changes rates after a feature launch. What metadata helps explain historical estimates?','Model and rate-effective version','Only the browser locale','Only the answer’s last sentence','Only the user’s display color','Historical accounting needs the price/model assumptions that applied to the relevant usage.','Locale does not identify the rate.','Narrative does not record commercial terms.','Colors do not define price versions.']
]},
{slug:'economic-controls',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Balance quality, latency and spending controls',before:'Use the token-budget and cost-ledger lessons. Distinguish an optimization hypothesis from measured evidence.',intro:'A retail assistant can answer routine product questions cheaply, but a difficult case may need a more capable model or a human. Cost controls should preserve useful behavior rather than blindly shorten every answer.',sections:[S('Optimize the whole request path','Measure task quality alongside latency and cost on a representative set. Smaller context, shorter output, cheaper models, caching and batching can help appropriate workloads, but each has assumptions. A cache must respect identity/data scope and freshness; batching may increase wait time.','A routing policy can use explicit task characteristics and a reviewed fallback. Do not infer that an expensive model is always better or that a cheap model is always sufficient. Evaluate the same task distribution and track failure or escalation rates.'),S('Enforce limits before runaway work','Use account budgets, per-operation output bounds and limits on retries or tool loops. A budget must govern total work, not only a single successful call. Provide a truthful limit-reached state and recovery path rather than fabricating an answer.','Compare optimization changes with a fixed baseline before deployment and observe real outcomes after a controlled release. Token savings alone do not establish product improvement. The fixture admits work under a synthetic remaining-currency budget and makes no model-quality claim.')],problem:'Apply a synthetic per-account budget across proposed request estimates.',code:'from decimal import Decimal\nremaining = Decimal("0.010")\naccepted = []\nfor estimate in [Decimal("0.004"), Decimal("0.005"), Decimal("0.003")]:\n    allow = estimate <= remaining\n    accepted.append(allow)\n    if allow:\n        remaining -= estimate\nprint(accepted)\nprint(f"{remaining:.3f}")',expect:'[True, True, False]\n0.001',steps:['Begin with a declared synthetic spending allowance.','Reserve accepted estimates before admitting more work.','Reject the request that exceeds the remaining allowance.'],limit:'This sequential estimate ledger does not prove concurrent budget reservation, invoice settlement or model quality. A production system needs atomic reservations, actual-usage reconciliation and explicit failure/release semantics.',mistakes:['Caching private responses without user/tenant scope.','Optimizing cost while ignoring task quality and failure rates.'],takeaways:['Measure quality, latency and cost together.','Bound aggregate work and reconcile reservations.'],refs:['tokens','retry'],questions:[
['What should accompany a cost-optimization metric?','Task quality and latency','Only a shorter answer','Only a cheaper model name','Only a higher retry count','Useful optimization balances product outcomes with time and resource costs.','Brevity can lose required information.','A model name is not outcome evidence.','Retries can increase work.'],
['Why can batching be unsuitable for interactive chat?','Added waiting time','It always breaks JSON','It always costs more','It removes user permissions','Batching may trade lower unit overhead for a less responsive interaction.','Batching need not change syntax.','Cost depends on the contract.','Authorization still applies.'],
['What does the budget fixture establish?','Sequential estimate admission','Atomic concurrent reservations','Provider invoice settlement','A model-quality improvement','The local loop checks admission against declared estimates in sequence.','No concurrent store is exercised.','No invoice is queried.','No generated answer is evaluated.'],
['A cache reuses one tenant’s private answer for another tenant. What must change?','Cache authorization and scope','Only the sampling temperature','Only the output length','Only the response font','Cost-saving caches must preserve access boundaries and appropriate key scope.','Sampling does not isolate cached data.','Shorter disclosures remain disclosures.','Presentation does not authorize reuse.'],
['A tool loop makes many individually cheap calls. What should the budget bound?','Total operation work','Only each call’s output','Only the first request','Only visible final text','Aggregate limits must account for repeated calls, retries and tool-enabled work.','Small individual calls can accumulate.','Later calls still consume resources.','Hidden work may be billable.'],
['A cheaper model passes routine cases but fails rare critical cases. What evidence matters?','Segmented task evaluation','Only average token savings','Only its vendor ranking','Only a single successful demo','The decision needs quality evidence for the important case distribution, including critical segments.','Savings do not establish adequacy.','Rankings are not this task’s evaluation.','One demo misses rare failures.'],
['Two requests simultaneously reserve the same remaining allowance. What property is missing?','Atomic budget coordination','A different currency symbol','A shorter prompt template','A renamed model adapter','Concurrent admission must not spend the same remaining capacity twice.','Display symbols do not coordinate writes.','Prompt length does not make reservations atomic.','Names do not enforce state transitions.'],
['A spending limit blocks assistance. Which UI behavior is appropriate?','State the limit and next step','Invent a completed answer','Hide the failed operation','Claim the provider is always down','The application should truthfully distinguish policy limits from completed work or provider availability.','Invented output misrepresents success.','Users need a recovery path.','A budget denial is not proof of an outage.']
]}
]);
